An abstract search can return something more useful than ``no
counterexample found'': a set of classes closed under successor
edges and disjoint from the potentially bad classes. A separate
checker can verify this set without trusting the search order or
its pruning heuristics. Assume the finite may graph already has a
justified abstraction of all relevant concrete transitions, and
the initial and bad markings are conservative. Graph certification
does not, by itself, establish either of those assumptions.

\begin{sudocode}
\Require May graph $G$, proposed safe region $R$
\To{Even an unreachable-looking initial class must be covered.}
\If{$\operatorname{initial}(G)\not\subseteq R$}
  \State \Return reject
\EndIf
\If{$R\cap\operatorname{bad}(G)\ne\varnothing$}
  \State \Return reject
\EndIf
\ForAll{edges $a\to b$ in $G$}
  \To{Only edges leaving a member constrain closure.}
  \If{$a\in R$ and $b\notin R$}
    \State \Return reject
  \EndIf
\EndFor
\State \Return certified safe in $G$
\end{sudocode}

For the request controller, include both initial request classes,
their chosen arms, the two join classes $(0,0)$ and $(1,1)$,
and the grant class with $r=1$. Include any non-grant exit reached
from $(0,0)$ as well. This region has no grant with $r=0$ and
is closed in the refined graph. Delete the grant class with $r=1$
from the proposed region. Although the revised region still
contains no bad class, the edge from $(1,1)$ to the omitted
grant class makes the certificate fail. Conversely, merely adding
grant with $r=0$ fails the bad-class check even if that class is
unreachable.

The proof invariant is membership in $R$ along every projected
execution prefix: initial coverage provides the base case and
closure provides the step. With bit-set membership this audit
is linear in the explicit graph and markings. It may accept a
region larger than the exact reachable set, which is fine for
safety. It is not a certificate of progress or termination;
a contained cycle can remain reachable forever. If refinement
changes the graph or the observation predicate, recheck the
old region only after mapping its classes into the new graph.

\section{A Refinement Loop with Explicit Outcomes}

Counterexample-guided abstraction refinement alternates three operations: search the current abstract graph, check an alleged bad path against the concrete transition relation, and, if the path is infeasible, choose information that blocks the reason for infeasibility. The path check must include actual entry constraints and the actual bad predicate at the end. A satisfying valuation yields a concrete witness to replay; an unsatisfiable formula can yield a cut predicate from a conflicting set of constraints or an interpolant shared between prefix and suffix. A solver timeout is neither an infeasibility proof nor a violation. When precision is iteratively added, check that the new graph still covers all concrete steps, including steps at points not mentioned in the counterexample. The process need not terminate on an infinite-state model or with an insufficient predicate language. For finite Boolean obligations, Chapter~\ref{ch:ext-217} shows how to separate the solver's search from checking its answer.

The dossier's \emph{Counterexample-Guided Compiler Synthesis} uses a related loop but changes rewrite candidates in response to concrete disagreements. Here the program stays fixed and the verifier changes its model in response to spurious disagreements. In either loop, store the observation predicate with the artifact: changing what counts as bad can invalidate an earlier result without changing a single program instruction.
